WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.351 --> 00:00:01.750
This is the Balancer.

2
00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:06.564
It caps how much of an exchange account any one coin is allowed to become.

3
00:00:06.564 --> 00:00:12.149
When a coin grows past its cap, Cyrus sells the excess and brings it back down.

4
00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:13.814
Why bother?

5
00:00:13.814 --> 00:00:18.415
When one coin rallies, it can quietly grow into most of your account.

6
00:00:18.415 --> 00:00:22.378
A cap takes some profit along the way and keeps your risk spread out.

7
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.477
Caps are set per account, and this menu picks which one.

8
00:00:26.477 --> 00:00:29.190
Everything on the page is that account alone.

9
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.637
Here it's the Kraken account called Main.

10
00:00:31.637 --> 00:00:35.161
The Balancer works with every exchange Cyrus supports.

11
00:00:35.847 --> 00:00:39.458
Switch accounts, and you see that account's own holdings

12
00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:42.167
and caps, measured against its own total.

13
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:43.536
Let's go back to Main.

14
00:00:44.216 --> 00:00:48.834
Each bar is one holding's share of the account, by its value in dollars.

15
00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:51.054
The account total is underneath.

16
00:00:51.729 --> 00:00:56.546
A capped coin has a handle at its maximum share, labelled with the percent.

17
00:00:56.546 --> 00:01:00.216
The dashed line is the down to level, where a trim stops.

18
00:01:00.216 --> 00:01:03.993
Below the bar is its share now, and the coin it sells into.

19
00:01:04.671 --> 00:01:08.717
When a coin is at or over its cap, the bar turns amber.

20
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:14.169
Bitcoin here is almost forty two percent of the account, against a cap of thirty.

21
00:01:14.832 --> 00:01:18.638
Fit zooms the scale in, which helps when your caps are small.

22
00:01:18.638 --> 00:01:21.591
Zero to a hundred shows the whole range again.

23
00:01:22.263 --> 00:01:24.128
Here's exactly what happens.

24
00:01:24.128 --> 00:01:27.658
On each check, about once a minute, Cyrus prices the

25
00:01:27.658 --> 00:01:31.189
account in dollars and works out each coin's share.

26
00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:36.149
If a coin has reached its cap, it sells enough to bring it down to the down to level.

27
00:01:36.835 --> 00:01:42.628
For bitcoin, that's from about forty two percent down to twenty five: roughly seventeen

28
00:01:42.628 --> 00:01:48.421
thousand dollars, or about a quarter of a bitcoin, sold into USDC with a market order.

29
00:01:49.103 --> 00:01:50.675
Why stop below the cap?

30
00:01:50.675 --> 00:01:55.542
Trimming only to the cap would let the next small rise set off another sale.

31
00:01:55.542 --> 00:02:01.340
The gap stops it trading back and forth, so the down to level must be lower than the maximum.

32
00:02:02.034 --> 00:02:06.933
The Positions table holds the same caps as numbers, alongside your holdings,

33
00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:11.253
their value, their share now, and what Cyrus would trim right now.

34
00:02:11.933 --> 00:02:17.880
Would Trim Now uses the same sums as the engine, so it's an honest preview of the next check.

35
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.205
Ether is under its cap, so nothing would be sold.

36
00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:24.156
You can drag a cap's handle on the chart.

37
00:02:24.156 --> 00:02:28.318
Pull ether's cap below its current share, and it's over straight away.

38
00:02:28.318 --> 00:02:33.638
If the down to level would no longer sit below the cap, Cyrus moves it down for you.

39
00:02:34.332 --> 00:02:38.005
The table follows along, with a trim that would now happen.

40
00:02:38.005 --> 00:02:40.918
You can also type the numbers into the table instead.

41
00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.762
To cap a coin that has none, tick its box.

42
00:02:44.762 --> 00:02:49.785
Cyrus suggests a cap about five points above where it sits today, a down

43
00:02:49.785 --> 00:02:54.948
to level five points lower, and, where it can, a stablecoin to sell into.

44
00:02:55.612 --> 00:03:00.297
Convert Into offers dollars, the main stablecoins, bitcoin and ether,

45
00:03:00.297 --> 00:03:04.711
but only the ones this exchange can actually trade the coin for.

46
00:03:05.395 --> 00:03:07.406
Untick a box to pause a cap.

47
00:03:07.406 --> 00:03:11.342
Its numbers are kept, but it won't trade until you tick it again.

48
00:03:11.342 --> 00:03:14.587
To remove a cap completely, clear its maximum.

49
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.791
Below are three safety settings, shared by every cap on this account.

50
00:03:19.791 --> 00:03:24.033
Cooldown is the shortest time between two trims of the same coin.

51
00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:27.611
It starts at one day, fourteen hundred and forty minutes,

52
00:03:27.611 --> 00:03:31.001
so a choppy market can't make it trade over and over.

53
00:03:31.687 --> 00:03:36.823
Minimum trade skips a trim worth less than this, twenty five dollars to start with.

54
00:03:36.823 --> 00:03:40.180
Raise it to five thousand, and the solana trim, worth

55
00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.219
about three thousand, is now under the minimum.

56
00:03:43.904 --> 00:03:47.325
With Simulate Only, Cyrus places no orders.

57
00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:51.194
It writes what it would have traded into the Automations log instead.

58
00:03:51.194 --> 00:03:56.301
It's a sensible way to watch the Balancer for a while before trusting it with real money.

59
00:03:56.983 --> 00:03:59.765
Nothing changes until you click Save Balancer.

60
00:03:59.765 --> 00:04:04.223
Saving replaces this account's whole set of caps: caps on the page

61
00:04:04.223 --> 00:04:08.276
are created or updated, and any cap you cleared is removed.

62
00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:13.182
Cyrus checks every cap first: the down to level must be below

63
00:04:13.182 --> 00:04:17.555
the maximum, and the exchange must have a market to sell into.

64
00:04:17.555 --> 00:04:20.800
If not, it names the coin and saves nothing.

65
00:04:21.476 --> 00:04:25.881
Discard Changes throws your edits away and reloads what's saved.

66
00:04:25.881 --> 00:04:28.740
We'll do that now, since this is only a demo.

67
00:04:29.445 --> 00:04:33.305
Once saved, each cap is an automation like any other.

68
00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:38.506
It's checked on the engine's regular cycle, listed on the Automations page, every

69
00:04:38.506 --> 00:04:43.451
trim or skip goes in the log, and trims are emailed to you if alerts are on.

70
00:04:44.144 --> 00:04:47.804
The refresh button reloads your holdings from the exchange.

71
00:04:48.474 --> 00:04:49.767
That's the Balancer.

72
00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:54.056
For each coin, choose a maximum, a level to come back down to,

73
00:04:54.056 --> 00:04:58.344
and what to sell into, and Cyrus keeps each account in shape.

